.TH HD-UP 1 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-up \- upgrade the system after a ZFS snapshot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-up
.RB [ \-\-unattended ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-up
upgrades the system with
.BR apt (8),
after a snapshot of the root dataset, so an upgrade that went wrong can be
undone with
.BR "zfs rollback" .
.IP 1. 4
It updates the package lists.
.IP 2. 4
It shows the packages that can be upgraded and those that the upgrade
removes, and stops when there are none.
.IP 3. 4
It asks whether to go on; anything but
.B y
stops it.
.IP 4. 4
It downloads the packages, so a download that fails leaves no snapshot
behind.
.IP 5. 4
It takes the snapshot
.BI rpool/hyprdebian@hd-up- date _ time\fR,
such as
.BR rpool/hyprdebian@hd-up-2026-10-17_143000 ,
and stops when that fails.
The snapshot has the property
.BR org.hyprdebian:hd-up ,
.B manual
or
.BR unattended .
.IP 6. 4
It runs
.BR "apt full-upgrade" ,
so upgrades that need new packages or remove old ones are not held back.
.IP 7. 4
After an upgrade that succeeded, it destroys the oldest
.B hd-up-
snapshots, so only the newest are kept; 8, unless the configuration says
otherwise, see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Other snapshots, such as
.BR @install ,
are left alone.
.IP 8. 4
It runs
.BR "hd-reboot-check \-\-write" ,
which writes
.I /run/reboot-required
when the new kernel or ZFS module needs a reboot, and says why; also when
everything was current.
.PP
Run it as a user who may use
.BR sudo (8),
or as root; as a user it runs itself again with
.BR sudo .
Only one
.B hd-up
runs at a time.
.SS Unattended upgrades
.B hd-up.timer
runs
.B hd-up.service
every Tuesday at 04:30, or after the next boot when the system was off
then. The service runs
.BR "hd-up \-\-unattended" ,
which does not ask, keeps the configuration files that were changed, and
upgrades nothing when:
.IP \(bu 4
the system runs on battery power;
.IP \(bu 4
the pool
.B rpool
is not
.BR ONLINE ,
such as with a missing drive;
.IP \(bu 4
.B dpkg \-\-audit
shows packages that are not fully installed;
.IP \(bu 4
the upgrade removes packages. Run
.B hd-up
by hand for such an upgrade, which shows the packages first.
.PP
In all but the first case the service fails, so the failed unit shows
after a login and on the dashboard. It never reboots: a new kernel takes
effect at the next reboot. Until then the message after a login, the
dashboard and a notification on the desktop say that a reboot is due, see
.BR hd-reboot-check (1).
.PP
Show the last run, or switch the weekly upgrades off and on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl \-u hd\-up
sudo systemctl disable \-\-now hd\-up.timer
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now hd\-up.timer
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run
.B sudo systemctl edit hd\-up.timer
to upgrade at another time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-unattended
Upgrade without asking, after checking the system, as described above.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The key
.B hd-up
in
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
holds the settings, set with
.BR hd-config-update (1):
.TP
.B keep
The number of
.B hd-up-
snapshots to keep, 1 or more; 8 without it.
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hd\-config\-update hd\-up '{"keep": 12}'
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the system is up to date, was upgraded, or the upgrade was
cancelled, 1 when an unattended upgrade was not started or another
.B hd-up
is running, otherwise the exit status of the command that failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Undo the last upgrade, after a reboot into a system that still works, or
from a live system:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zfs list \-t snapshot rpool/hyprdebian
zfs rollback rpool/hyprdebian@hd\-up\-2026\-10\-17_143000
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd\-up.timer
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd\-up.service
The weekly unattended upgrade.
.TP
.I /run/hd\-up.lock
The lock that keeps a second
.B hd-up
from running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR hd-config-update (1),
.BR hd-reboot-check (1),
.BR apt (8),
.BR zfs-rollback (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Erik Van Hamme <erik.vanhamme@gmail.com>
